    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "...You should also try running the VPN client while connected via a completely different network, such as using your mobile phone's hotspot instead of your usual office or home Wi-Fi. This crucial step helps isolate the variable: if the VPN connects successfully over the hotspot, the fault clearly lies within your primary local network environment, such as a router setting or an ISP-level restriction. When it fails across both connections, the issue is more likely rooted on the client machine or the VPN server itself.\n\nFurthermore, examine your network adapter settings; for more advanced users, you may consider temporarily flushing your local DNS cache by opening the Command Prompt as an administrator and executing the command `ipconfig /flushdns`. DNS (Domain Name System) is essentially the phonebook of the internet, translating human-readable server names (like `vpn.omnicorp.com`) into numerical IP addresses that computers actually use to find each other; a corrupted local cache can prevent your machine from finding the correct, current address of the VPN gateway.\n\nIf these steps have proven ineffective, the next area of investigation involves checking proxy server configurations within your operating system settings. In a corporate environment, network traffic often routes through specific proxies, acting as intermediaries for all data",
